RUNBOOK - api-unreachable  -  printed procedures with your cluster's names filled in - kdash never runs them
runbooks:   1 etcd-restore    2 cert-rotate    3 node-notready   [4 api-unreachable]   5 oidc-login    [ ] previous / next   (kdash --runbook NAME)

WHEN TO USE  -  kubectl / kdash cannot reach the API server (https://localhost:10093)

  STEP 1  from this host
      read   kubectl --context vcluster-docker_dev cluster-info  # the exact error: refused, timeout, x509, Unauthorized
      read   curl -sk https://localhost:10093/readyz; echo  # any answer (even Unauthorized) means the network path and TLS work
      read   timeout 4 openssl s_client -connect localhost:10093 </dev/null 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate  # certificate expired? (then: kdash --runbook cert-rotate)
      note   Unauthorized / login prompt that fails: kdash --runbook oidc-login

  STEP 2  on a server
      read   [on dev] sudo systemctl status rke2-server --no-pager
      read   [on dev] sudo journalctl -u rke2-server --since -30min --no-pager | tail -n 100  # etcd quorum, certificate or disk errors
      read   [on dev] sudo /var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/crictl --runtime-endpoint unix:///run/k3s/containerd/containerd.sock ps | grep -E 'kube-apiserver|etcd'  # are the API server and etcd containers running?
      read   [on dev] sudo /var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/kubectl --kubeconfig /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml get --raw '/readyz?verbose'  # which API check fails, bypassing the load balancer and OIDC

  STEP 3  recover
      CHANGE [on dev] sudo systemctl restart rke2-server  # one server at a time; etcd needs a majority up (1 of 1)
      note   if etcd lost quorum or its data is corrupt: kdash --runbook etcd-restore
